1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,283
Here's a brand-new computer in the cloud.

2
00:00:02,603 --> 00:00:05,803
It's ours, and we have root,
full administrator rights.

3
00:00:06,123 --> 00:00:09,600
We ask pip for two Python packages,
and pip says no.

4
00:00:10,083 --> 00:00:14,648
This command isn't from the README;
it's the one most of us would type first.

5
00:00:14,968 --> 00:00:18,373
The error says
the environment is externally managed,

6
00:00:18,373 --> 00:00:20,622
and suggests a virtual environment.

7
00:00:20,942 --> 00:00:24,163
By the end of this video,
every line of it will make sense.

8
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:28,015
Today's lecture uses the word virtual twice.

9
00:00:28,335 --> 00:00:32,132
A virtual machine is a whole computer
you rent and throw away.

10
00:00:32,452 --> 00:00:37,529
A virtual environment is a private set
of Python packages for one project.

11
00:00:37,849 --> 00:00:39,727
And one will live inside the other.

12
00:00:39,967 --> 00:00:44,295
The tutorial, simple-translate,
runs one small app four ways,

13
00:00:44,295 --> 00:00:46,708
each removing a kind of fragility.

14
00:00:47,028 --> 00:00:49,713
Today we climb the first two rungs:

15
00:00:49,713 --> 00:00:55,391
a manual install on a cloud VM,
then a fresh VM of the same kind, with uv.

16
00:00:55,711 --> 00:00:57,439
Docker comes next lecture.

17
00:00:57,683 --> 00:01:00,926
To follow along,
open the README beside this video.

18
00:01:01,246 --> 00:01:03,976
The tracker,
top left, shows the README step.

19
00:01:04,296 --> 00:01:06,814
Code panels show the file and line numbers.

20
00:01:07,134 --> 00:01:12,040
When the yellow your-turn tag appears,
pause and run the command on your own VM.

21
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:16,181
A grey extra tag marks a command
that isn't in the README;

22
00:01:16,181 --> 00:01:18,355
extras are safe, so run them too.

23
00:01:18,675 --> 00:01:23,326
Every command is also in the study guide,
in the MLOps Learning repo.

24
00:01:23,567 --> 00:01:25,880
Before you start, finish Tutorial 0,

25
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:29,156
so you have a Google Cloud
project with billing on.

26
00:01:29,476 --> 00:01:33,380
No service account or keys today;
all you need is a browser.

27
00:01:34,417 --> 00:01:37,596
Before we install anything,
let's see what we're installing.

28
00:01:37,916 --> 00:01:42,481
Open cli.py on GitHub;
you'll clone it onto your VM soon.

29
00:01:42,801 --> 00:01:47,068
The first two imports, argparse and asyncio,
come with Python.

30
00:01:47,388 --> 00:01:50,033
The next two, art and googletrans, don't.

31
00:01:50,353 --> 00:01:51,953
They have to come from somewhere.

32
00:01:52,273 --> 00:01:55,004
This whole lecture
is about where they come from.

33
00:01:55,250 --> 00:01:59,695
The main function prints a banner
that says AC215 in ASCII art,

34
00:01:59,695 --> 00:02:03,506
then the parsed arguments,
then the text to translate.

35
00:02:03,826 --> 00:02:08,008
If you pass a file with dash f,
the text comes from that file instead.

36
00:02:08,250 --> 00:02:11,416
At the bottom,
argparse defines four options,

37
00:02:11,416 --> 00:02:13,316
and each one has a default:

38
00:02:13,316 --> 00:02:14,936
the text I love cheese,

39
00:02:14,936 --> 00:02:18,106
source language English,
destination Italian.

40
00:02:18,426 --> 00:02:24,485
So running cli.py with no options
translates I love cheese into Italian.

41
00:02:24,734 --> 00:02:27,980
The last line starts
an event loop and runs main,

42
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:31,027
because googletrans
version 4 is asynchronous.

43
00:02:31,347 --> 00:02:35,827
Inside main, a Translator sends
your text off with one awaited call.

44
00:02:36,067 --> 00:02:40,271
That call is a single
HTTPS request from the VM

45
00:02:40,271 --> 00:02:43,491
to translate dot googleapis dot com.

46
00:02:43,811 --> 00:02:49,763
It's Google's unofficial translate endpoint:
free, no API key, no promises.

47
00:02:50,083 --> 00:02:54,584
A little JSON comes back,
and line 39 prints the translation.

48
00:02:54,834 --> 00:02:59,869
One detail on line 25 matters later:
raise exception equals True.

49
00:03:00,189 --> 00:03:03,063
Without it,
when Google answers with an error,

50
00:03:03,063 --> 00:03:04,313
googletrans hides it

51
00:03:04,313 --> 00:03:07,371
and hands your own input back
as the translation,

52
00:03:07,371 --> 00:03:08,807
so nothing looks wrong.

53
00:03:09,127 --> 00:03:11,986
With it,
a failure becomes an error you can read.

54
00:03:12,234 --> 00:03:15,424
Lines 32 to 37 catch any error
from that call

55
00:03:15,424 --> 00:03:19,253
and wrap it in a friendlier
message about the network.

56
00:03:19,573 --> 00:03:20,895
Remember these lines.

57
00:03:21,215 --> 00:03:23,092
In a few minutes, they'll fool us.

58
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:27,837
So the app is small:
arguments in, one web request, one line out.

59
00:03:28,157 --> 00:03:30,781
What it needs is two third-party packages.

60
00:03:31,101 --> 00:03:33,576
Let's get a computer, and find them a home.

61
00:03:34,817 --> 00:03:37,784
A virtual machine
is a slice of a real server

62
00:03:37,784 --> 00:03:39,894
in one of Google's data centers.

63
00:03:40,214 --> 00:03:44,225
Software called a hypervisor
carves the machine into slices.

64
00:03:44,545 --> 00:03:48,310
Each slice gets virtual CPUs
and memory from that server,

65
00:03:48,310 --> 00:03:52,737
a disk from Google's storage network,
and its own operating system.

66
00:03:53,057 --> 00:03:54,636
One of them will be yours.

67
00:03:54,884 --> 00:03:59,719
In the console, check
that your Tutorial 0 project is selected,

68
00:03:59,719 --> 00:04:03,097
then open Compute Engine, then VM instances.

69
00:04:03,417 --> 00:04:07,666
The first time, it may ask you
to enable the Compute Engine API;

70
00:04:07,666 --> 00:04:09,924
click enable and give it a minute.

71
00:04:10,244 --> 00:04:11,844
Then click Create instance.

72
00:04:12,084 --> 00:04:15,455
The README gives no settings,
so here are sensible ones.

73
00:04:15,775 --> 00:04:18,676
A name, like ac215-l01.

74
00:04:18,996 --> 00:04:22,196
A region and zone:
where the machine physically lives.

75
00:04:22,516 --> 00:04:26,040
A machine type,
which sets the CPUs and memory;

76
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:27,465
e2-small is plenty.

77
00:04:27,785 --> 00:04:29,897
And a boot disk with Debian 12.

78
00:04:30,217 --> 00:04:33,801
Debian 13 works too;
we'll see where it changes things.

79
00:04:34,050 --> 00:04:37,378
Keep the external IP address;
it's on by default.

80
00:04:37,698 --> 00:04:40,464
Without it, and without extra network setup,

81
00:04:40,464 --> 00:04:44,802
this VM can't reach the internet,
and every download here would hang.

82
00:04:45,050 --> 00:04:46,607
And now the meter starts.

83
00:04:46,927 --> 00:04:52,858
CPU and memory are billed by the second
while the VM runs, even when it sits idle.

84
00:04:53,178 --> 00:04:55,631
The disk is billed for as long as it exists.

85
00:04:55,951 --> 00:04:59,578
The estimate is small,
but the meter never stops on its own.

86
00:04:59,834 --> 00:05:03,418
Click Create, wait for the green check,
then click SSH.

87
00:05:03,738 --> 00:05:07,215
If it asks you to authorize the connection,
click Authorize.

88
00:05:07,535 --> 00:05:11,375
A terminal opens in your browser,
logged in to your new VM.

89
00:05:11,695 --> 00:05:14,981
Our prompt says student;
yours shows your own username.

90
00:05:15,301 --> 00:05:17,796
Pause here, and get your VM to this point.

91
00:05:18,050 --> 00:05:20,823
Three quick extra checks, not in the README.

92
00:05:21,143 --> 00:05:23,789
First, which operating system did we get?

93
00:05:24,109 --> 00:05:28,120
Print the os-release file:
Debian 12, nicknamed bookworm.

94
00:05:28,384 --> 00:05:30,987
Second, ask python3 for its version.

95
00:05:31,307 --> 00:05:36,960
Debian 12 ships Python 3.11.2,
for the operating system itself.

96
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:40,992
On Debian 13, you'd see 3.13.5.

97
00:05:41,312 --> 00:05:42,976
Keep both numbers in mind.

98
00:05:43,234 --> 00:05:45,623
Third, which python, without the 3.

99
00:05:45,943 --> 00:05:48,930
The which command asks
which program a name would run.

100
00:05:49,250 --> 00:05:53,943
For python, nothing comes back:
Debian has python3, but no python.

101
00:05:54,263 --> 00:05:55,970
Remember this for part 2.

102
00:05:57,033 --> 00:05:59,550
Step 3: type sudo apt-get update.

103
00:05:59,870 --> 00:06:04,436
Debian's package manager is apt,
and sudo runs it as the administrator.

104
00:06:04,756 --> 00:06:07,100
Update downloads the package catalog:

105
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:10,836
the list of packages that exist,
and their latest versions.

106
00:06:11,156 --> 00:06:12,820
Nothing is installed yet.

107
00:06:13,140 --> 00:06:18,046
On a Google VM, you'll see a few more lines,
for Google's own package sources.

108
00:06:18,366 --> 00:06:19,518
Pause and run it.

109
00:06:19,766 --> 00:06:23,670
The README has a tip here:
apt-get and apt do the same job.

110
00:06:23,990 --> 00:06:27,325
The apt-get command is the stable,
scriptable one,

111
00:06:27,325 --> 00:06:29,793
for Dockerfiles and automated builds.

112
00:06:30,113 --> 00:06:33,035
The shorter apt is friendlier
for typing by hand.

113
00:06:33,355 --> 00:06:34,678
Either is fine here.

114
00:06:34,916 --> 00:06:37,305
Step 4: sudo apt install git.

115
00:06:37,625 --> 00:06:39,844
This time apt really installs things.

116
00:06:40,164 --> 00:06:43,556
It lists what it will add and asks,
do you want to continue?

117
00:06:43,876 --> 00:06:45,455
Type Y and press enter.

118
00:06:45,775 --> 00:06:50,020
The list depends on your VM;
if git is already there, apt says so.

119
00:06:50,340 --> 00:06:51,492
Pause and run it.

120
00:06:51,733 --> 00:06:53,610
Step 5: clone the repo.

121
00:06:53,930 --> 00:06:57,322
Type git clone, then the address;
copy it from the README.

122
00:06:57,642 --> 00:07:00,585
Git copies the whole repo, with its history,

123
00:07:00,585 --> 00:07:04,597
into a new folder, simple-translate,
in your home directory.

124
00:07:04,833 --> 00:07:07,244
Step 6: cd simple-translate.

125
00:07:07,564 --> 00:07:11,745
Watch the prompt: after the colon,
it now shows the folder you're in.

126
00:07:12,065 --> 00:07:14,604
The wavy tilde means your home folder.

127
00:07:14,924 --> 00:07:16,630
Pause, and catch up to here.

128
00:07:16,866 --> 00:07:19,575
One extra check: ls lists the files.

129
00:07:19,895 --> 00:07:21,041
There's cli.py,

130
00:07:21,041 --> 00:07:24,553
the README,
a Dockerfile saved as a text file,

131
00:07:24,553 --> 00:07:26,731
two scripts for next lecture,

132
00:07:26,731 --> 00:07:30,562
and pyproject.toml,
the list of what the app needs.

133
00:07:30,882 --> 00:07:36,386
Notice what's missing: no requirements file,
and no lock file with exact versions.

134
00:07:36,706 --> 00:07:37,922
Hold on to that.

135
00:07:39,167 --> 00:07:42,324
Step 7: sudo apt install python3-pip.

136
00:07:42,644 --> 00:07:46,570
That's Debian's own packaged copy of pip,
for Debian's Python.

137
00:07:46,890 --> 00:07:48,170
Answer Y again.

138
00:07:48,490 --> 00:07:53,375
Ours pulled in over a hundred packages;
a real VM usually needs fewer.

139
00:07:53,695 --> 00:07:54,847
Pause and run it.

140
00:07:55,084 --> 00:07:58,135
An extra check: ask pip3 for its version.

141
00:07:58,455 --> 00:08:01,679
It's pip 23.0.1, for Python 3.11,

142
00:08:01,679 --> 00:08:06,369
in a folder called dist-packages
under user lib.

143
00:08:06,689 --> 00:08:11,297
That user is spelled U-S-R:
the system's files, not your home folder.

144
00:08:11,534 --> 00:08:15,939
Now the command from the start of the video,
without sudo or any flag;

145
00:08:15,939 --> 00:08:17,827
it isn't in the README either.

146
00:08:18,147 --> 00:08:22,371
Again, pip refuses:
this environment is externally managed.

147
00:08:22,691 --> 00:08:26,851
The hint says: install with apt,
or create a virtual environment.

148
00:08:27,171 --> 00:08:29,966
Its last note even names a way around it.

149
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:31,267
Why refuse?

150
00:08:31,587 --> 00:08:33,805
Picture how Python finds an import.

151
00:08:34,125 --> 00:08:37,787
This extra one-liner prints
the list called sys dot path:

152
00:08:37,787 --> 00:08:39,587
the folders Python searches.

153
00:08:39,907 --> 00:08:41,336
Think of them as shelves.

154
00:08:41,656 --> 00:08:45,097
On every import,
Python scans from the top shelf down,

155
00:08:45,097 --> 00:08:46,755
and takes the first match.

156
00:08:47,967 --> 00:08:49,930
The bottom shelf belongs to apt.

157
00:08:50,250 --> 00:08:54,917
Debian itself runs Python tools
that depend on exactly those jars,

158
00:08:54,917 --> 00:08:56,756
at exactly those versions.

159
00:08:57,076 --> 00:09:00,831
The system Python is a shared kitchen,
and apt is the cook.

160
00:09:01,151 --> 00:09:03,242
That's what externally managed means.

161
00:09:03,562 --> 00:09:07,082
PEP 668 is the rule
that makes pip respect it.

162
00:09:07,317 --> 00:09:09,624
Step 8 is the README's command:

163
00:09:09,624 --> 00:09:13,717
sudo pip3 install,
with the flag break-system-packages.

164
00:09:14,037 --> 00:09:18,453
The flag overrides the guard;
sudo lets pip write as administrator.

165
00:09:18,773 --> 00:09:19,925
Pause and run it.

166
00:09:20,245 --> 00:09:22,670
We asked for 2 packages and got 12;

167
00:09:22,670 --> 00:09:27,520
if yours says requirement already satisfied,
apt already had that one.

168
00:09:27,840 --> 00:09:32,746
And the warning says running pip as root
can clash with the system's package manager.

169
00:09:32,984 --> 00:09:34,115
Where did they go?

170
00:09:34,435 --> 00:09:38,142
This extra one-liner asks googletrans
where it lives:

171
00:09:38,142 --> 00:09:43,459
in dist-packages under user local,
still the system's, shared by every user.

172
00:09:43,779 --> 00:09:47,256
Look at the shelves:
that shelf sits above the one apt owns.

173
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:52,556
Now suppose apt had installed an older httpx
for some system tool.

174
00:09:52,876 --> 00:09:55,965
Our googletrans needs 0.27.2 or newer,

175
00:09:55,965 --> 00:10:00,193
so pip would add a second copy,
on the higher shelf.

176
00:10:00,513 --> 00:10:04,469
Python would find that copy first
and hide the one from apt,

177
00:10:04,469 --> 00:10:08,492
for every program
that uses the system Python, not just ours.

178
00:10:08,812 --> 00:10:10,327
That's what the flag breaks.

179
00:10:10,567 --> 00:10:14,190
To be fair, the README
says it uses the flag deliberately,

180
00:10:14,190 --> 00:10:18,439
as a quick demonstration,
and the next walkthrough does it properly.

181
00:10:18,684 --> 00:10:20,028
Something to ponder.

182
00:10:20,348 --> 00:10:26,526
A classmate's project
on this VM needs httpx 0.13,

183
00:10:26,526 --> 00:10:29,863
and yours needs httpx 0.28.

184
00:10:30,183 --> 00:10:32,423
Both install system-wide, like this.

185
00:10:32,743 --> 00:10:33,745
What happens?

186
00:10:42,584 --> 00:10:46,808
There's only one shelf,
so there's room for only one httpx.

187
00:10:47,128 --> 00:10:51,608
The last install wins, for everyone,
and one of the two projects breaks.

188
00:10:53,150 --> 00:10:54,601
Step 9: run it.

189
00:10:54,921 --> 00:11:00,809
Type python3 cli.py, with the 3,
since plain python doesn't exist here.

190
00:11:01,129 --> 00:11:04,378
You should see the banner,
the arguments, your input,

191
00:11:04,378 --> 00:11:06,953
and the output: I love cheese, in Italian.

192
00:11:07,273 --> 00:11:10,734
If you get a long red error instead,
don't change anything;

193
00:11:10,734 --> 00:11:12,905
we'll read ours together in a minute.

194
00:11:13,225 --> 00:11:14,419
Pause and try it.

195
00:11:14,667 --> 00:11:16,608
Then the README's other two runs.

196
00:11:16,928 --> 00:11:20,479
Dash t gives the text,
dash s the source language,

197
00:11:20,479 --> 00:11:22,112
dash d the destination.

198
00:11:22,432 --> 00:11:26,443
Copy them from the README, quotes included,
and pause to run both.

199
00:11:26,763 --> 00:11:29,557
One comes back in Spanish,
the other in French.

200
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:34,252
Now, honestly: when we recorded this,
Google refused our requests,

201
00:11:34,252 --> 00:11:37,288
and the run ended
in this long red traceback.

202
00:11:37,608 --> 00:11:40,915
Let's read it; you'll meet tracebacks
like this all course long.

203
00:11:41,150 --> 00:11:43,625
This is a chained traceback: two errors.

204
00:11:43,945 --> 00:11:47,913
The line between them says
the first one directly caused the second.

205
00:11:48,233 --> 00:11:52,222
Most people read only the last line,
but here that's just the wrapper.

206
00:11:52,542 --> 00:11:55,166
Scroll up to the first error:
that's the cause.

207
00:11:55,400 --> 00:11:59,539
The cause is status code 429:
too many requests.

208
00:11:59,859 --> 00:12:03,687
Google's unofficial endpoint
was rate-limiting our machine,

209
00:12:03,687 --> 00:12:06,088
as it often does with cloud machines.

210
00:12:06,408 --> 00:12:08,435
Nothing on the VM was broken.

211
00:12:08,683 --> 00:12:13,279
The error on the last line
is the app's own wrapper,

212
00:12:13,279 --> 00:12:15,488
lines 32 to 37 of cli.py.

213
00:12:15,808 --> 00:12:19,083
It catches anything,
and raises a friendlier message,

214
00:12:19,083 --> 00:12:20,566
chained to the original.

215
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:22,272
Why read the first error?

216
00:12:22,592 --> 00:12:26,496
This extra command has a typo:
x x isn't a language.

217
00:12:26,816 --> 00:12:30,016
The first error says
invalid destination language.

218
00:12:30,336 --> 00:12:35,435
But the last line still blames the network,
because the same wrapper catches everything.

219
00:12:35,755 --> 00:12:38,485
A small flaw in the code, not in your setup.

220
00:12:38,805 --> 00:12:42,432
The valid codes are in a table
at the bottom of the README.

221
00:12:42,667 --> 00:12:45,329
If you hit a 429, wait and retry,

222
00:12:45,329 --> 00:12:48,960
try another region,
or run it on your laptop.

223
00:12:49,280 --> 00:12:53,430
If you got as far as the Input line,
the imports worked;

224
00:12:53,430 --> 00:12:57,579
when the first error is a 429,
only the network said no.

225
00:12:58,817 --> 00:13:01,654
Step 10, the last one: delete the VM.

226
00:13:01,974 --> 00:13:06,582
As the README says,
a running VM keeps charging, even when idle.

227
00:13:06,902 --> 00:13:09,313
So why delete it, and not just stop it?

228
00:13:09,550 --> 00:13:12,615
Stopping ends the billing
for CPU and memory,

229
00:13:12,615 --> 00:13:14,386
but the disk still exists,

230
00:13:14,386 --> 00:13:18,148
so it keeps billing,
and so would a reserved IP address.

231
00:13:18,400 --> 00:13:23,029
Deleting removes the VM,
and by default its boot disk goes with it.

232
00:13:23,349 --> 00:13:27,232
That's a setting on the boot disk,
and it's on unless you changed it.

233
00:13:27,552 --> 00:13:28,917
And the meter stops.

234
00:13:29,237 --> 00:13:32,928
Select your instance, click Delete,
and pause until it's gone.

235
00:13:33,167 --> 00:13:37,754
Then check two pages:
VM instances and Disks should both be empty.

236
00:13:38,074 --> 00:13:42,808
With the gcloud tool on your laptop,
two extra commands do the same:

237
00:13:42,808 --> 00:13:47,055
gcloud compute instances list,
and gcloud compute disks list.

238
00:13:47,375 --> 00:13:49,103
Neither is in the README.

239
00:13:49,350 --> 00:13:51,099
That was the quick and dirty way.

240
00:13:51,419 --> 00:13:55,707
It worked, but it changed the system Python
for every program on the machine.

241
00:13:56,027 --> 00:13:59,846
In part 2: the same app,
on a fresh VM, the clean way.

